From cd5b86ad17710d9d399e6f73626d68b497e78ac9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:19:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] fix(validator): judge a session under the revision its
 server negotiated
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A session that completed an `initialize` handshake runs under the revision
the server's result names — that is what negotiation means. `declared::select`
judged every supported revision the session touched instead, so a client
proposing `2025-11-25` to a server answering `2026-07-28` drew a side-by-side
report, with a `2025-11-25` `LIFE-003` failure for an `initialized` the
session never owed; and a server answering `2024-11-05`, which this build
cannot judge, was silently dropped from the set and the session judged under
the proposal as a `pass`.

One rule now. With a completed handshake, the negotiated revision is the one
judged, alone; when the client proposed another, `validate` says so on stderr
(`note: the client proposed protocol revision …; the server's initialize
result negotiated …`), and `--revision` still judges the proposal on request.
A negotiated revision no registry describes is the unknown-revision refusal
(exit 2), now naming the negotiation and the proposal, never an approximation
under the proposal. A handshake answered with an error negotiated nothing (the
retry is the handshake), one a protected resource refused before reading it
neither, and a malformed `protocolVersion` is `LIFE-006`'s finding, not a
revision. Without a handshake the per-request `_meta` and header
declarations are judged as before, so `2026-07-28` sessions are unchanged:
no existing golden moved.

`Selection` gains `proposed`; `UnjudgeableRevisions` gains `negotiated` and
`proposed` (both `#[non_exhaustive]`). Two corpus traces pin the rule — a
`2025-11-25` client negotiated down to `2024-11-05` (refused by default,
passing under `--revision 2025-11-25`, which its golden is) and one
negotiated up to `2026-07-28` (one report, under which the legacy
`initialize` is a request without its `_meta`, BASE-030) — with ledger rows.

